"""Saint-of-the-day entries for Ora, March, April and May (original text). See tools/SAINTS_BRIEF.md."""
SAINTS_NEW = {
 "03-04": dict(name="Saint Casimir", dates="1458–1484 · Kraków and Vilnius", match=["casimir"],
  history="A prince of Poland and Lithuania, son of King Casimir IV, who lived at the heart of a powerful court as quietly as a monk. He prayed through the night, gave freely to the poor, loved Our Lady and refused a royal marriage to remain chaste. He died of tuberculosis at twenty-five and is the patron of Lithuania.",
  life="Casimir was the second son of Casimir IV Jagiellon and Elizabeth of Austria, and was taught by the historian Jan Długosz. In 1471, at thirteen, his father sent him with an army to take the crown of Hungary, offered by nobles opposed to King Matthias Corvinus. The venture failed and Casimir came home, and he seems never to have wanted a throne again. For a time he governed Poland while his father was in Lithuania, and he was known for fairness and for his care of the poor. He slept little, often praying at church doors before they opened, and wore a hair shirt beneath his fine clothes. He declined a proposed marriage to a daughter of the Emperor. He died at Grodno on 4 March 1484 and was buried in Vilnius Cathedral with a copy of the Marian hymn Omni die dic Mariae, which he loved.",
  prayerTo="Saint Casimir, you lived among wealth and power and kept your heart for God alone. Teach me to use what I have for the poor, to guard my purity, and to love the Mother of God with a simple and faithful love. Pray for Lithuania and Poland, and for all who carry responsibility for others. Amen.",
  penance="Casimir gave his princely income to the poor. Give today what you would have spent on a treat, and pray one Hail Mary for each person you pass who looks in need.",
  meditation="He had every comfort a court could give and chose to kneel at the church door before dawn. What comfort keeps you from prayer?",
  query="Saint Casimir painting Carlo Dolci"),
 "03-07": dict(name="Saints Perpetua and Felicity", dates="Died 203 · Carthage", match=["perpetua"],
  history="Two young mothers martyred together in the amphitheatre at Carthage: Perpetua, a well-born woman of twenty-two nursing her baby son, and Felicity, a slave who gave birth in prison days before she died. Perpetua kept a prison diary, one of the earliest writings we have by a Christian woman. Both are named in the Roman Canon.",
  life="In 203, under the Emperor Septimius Severus, a group of catechumens was arrested at Carthage: Perpetua, the slaves Felicity and Revocatus, Saturninus and Secundulus, and later their teacher Saturus, who gave himself up to share their fate. They were baptised while under guard. Perpetua’s father, a pagan, came again and again to beg her to sacrifice for the sake of her child. She pointed to a water jug and asked whether it could be called anything but what it was; nor, she said, could she be called anything but a Christian. In prison she recorded visions, among them a narrow ladder to heaven guarded by a serpent. Felicity, eight months pregnant, feared she would be left behind, since the law forbade the execution of pregnant women; she gave birth to a daughter, who was taken in by a Christian woman. On 7 March they were sent into the arena with wild beasts and then put to the sword, giving each other the kiss of peace.",
  prayerTo="Saints Perpetua and Felicity, noblewoman and slave, you went into the arena side by side, sisters in Christ. Pray for mothers and their children, for all who suffer for the faith, and for me, that I may never be ashamed to be called what I am, a Christian. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for Christians in prison for their faith, and for mothers who are separated from their children. If you can, give to a charity that supports either.",
  meditation="Could this jug be called by any other name? So I cannot be called anything but what I am, a Christian. Where are you tempted to answer to another name?",
  query="Saints Perpetua and Felicity painting"),
 "03-08": dict(name="Saint John of God", dates="1495–1550 · Portugal and Granada", match=["john of god"],
  history="A Portuguese shepherd, soldier and bookseller who, moved to tears by a sermon of Saint John of Ávila, gave everything away and was locked up as a madman. He came out to found a hospital for the sick poor of Granada, and his followers became the Brothers Hospitallers. He is a patron of hospitals, nurses and the sick.",
  life="João Cidade left his home at Montemor-o-Novo as a boy of eight and grew up in Spain, working as a shepherd near Oropesa. He served as a soldier, fought against the Turks at Vienna, and wandered for years, working in North Africa and then selling books in Gibraltar and Granada. In Granada, around 1537, he heard John of Ávila preach and was so overcome with sorrow for his sins that he tore up his books and ran through the streets crying for mercy. He was put in the Royal Hospital among the insane, and saw how the sick were treated there. John of Ávila steered his zeal toward service. He rented a house, carried the sick to it on his back, and begged for them at night, calling out, ‘Do good, brothers, to yourselves.’ He died on 8 March 1550, weakened after trying to save a drowning man.",
  prayerTo="Saint John of God, you were thought a fool for Christ and became a father to the sick and the forgotten. Pray for the sick, for those with troubled minds, and for all who nurse them. Give me eyes to see the suffering I would rather pass by, and hands ready to help. Amen.",
  penance="Visit or call someone who is sick or alone today, or give to a hospital, hospice or night shelter. John begged for the poor; ask someone else to give with you.",
  meditation="Do good, brothers, to yourselves. Whatever you give to the poor, he taught, you give to your own soul. Whom will you do good to today?",
  query="Saint John of God Murillo"),
 "03-09": dict(name="Saint Frances of Rome", dates="1384–1440 · Rome", match=["frances"],
  history="A Roman noblewoman who wanted to be a nun but was married at twelve, and became a saint as wife, mother and widow. Through plague, war and the deaths of two of her children she nursed the sick and fed the poor of Rome. She founded the Oblates of Tor de’ Specchi and is said to have seen her guardian angel.",
  life="Francesca Bussa de’ Leoni was married to Lorenzo Ponziani, of a rich Roman family, and lived with her husband for forty years in deep affection. With her sister-in-law Vannozza she visited hospitals, nursed the sick and gave away food from the family stores, to the anger of her father-in-law until, tradition says, the granary was found full again. Rome was torn by the Great Schism and occupied by the troops of Ladislas of Naples; the Ponziani lost their property, Lorenzo was wounded and their son Battista was taken hostage. Two of her children died in plague years. In 1425 she founded a community of women living under the Benedictine Olivetans, without enclosure, serving the poor of the city. When Lorenzo died in 1436 she joined them as superior. She is the patron of motorists, from the tradition that her angel lit her way at night.",
  prayerTo="Saint Frances, you sought God in the chapel and found Him also in the kitchen, the sickroom and the streets of Rome. Pray for wives, mothers and widows, for families in hardship, and for me, that I may serve God faithfully in the duties of my state of life. Amen.",
  penance="When a household duty interrupts your prayer today, leave the prayer gladly and do it well. Give some food to a food bank in honour of Frances.",
  meditation="She was often called away from prayer by her family, and tradition says that once she came back to find the page she had left written in gold. Which interruption today is God’s?",
  query="Saint Frances of Rome angel painting Gentileschi"),
 "03-17": dict(name="Saint Patrick", dates="Fifth century · Britain and Ireland", match=["patrick"],
  history="A Briton taken as a slave to Ireland at sixteen, who escaped, became a priest and went back to the people who had enslaved him. As bishop he preached through much of Ireland, baptised thousands and ordained clergy. His own Confession, written in plain, humble Latin, tells his story. He is the patron of Ireland.",
  life="Patrick was born in Roman Britain, the son of a deacon, Calpurnius, and grandson of a priest. He tells us that he did not know the true God when Irish raiders carried him off. For six years he herded animals, and in hunger and cold he began to pray, up to a hundred times a day and as often at night. A voice in a dream told him a ship was ready, and he walked some two hundred miles to find it. Home again, he dreamed of a man bringing letters, ‘the voice of the Irish’, begging him to come and walk among them once more. He was trained and ordained, and returned as a bishop. In his Letter to Coroticus he condemned a British chieftain whose men had killed and enslaved newly baptised converts. The shamrock and the banished snakes are later legend; the Confession shows a man astonished at God’s mercy.",
  prayerTo="Saint Patrick, you forgave those who enslaved you and brought them the faith. Pray for Ireland and her people throughout the world, for all held in slavery today, and for me, that Christ may be before me, behind me and within me, and that I may never be ashamed of Him. Amen.",
  prayer="Christ be with me, Christ within me, Christ behind me, Christ before me, Christ beside me, Christ to win me, Christ to comfort and restore me. Christ beneath me, Christ above me, Christ in quiet, Christ in danger, Christ in hearts of all that love me, Christ in mouth of friend and stranger.",
  prayerSource="Saint Patrick’s Breastplate, tr. Cecil Frances Alexander, 1889",
  penance="Patrick was himself a slave. Give today to a charity that works against human trafficking, and pray the Breastplate for someone held against their will.",
  meditation="I, Patrick, a sinner, the most unlearned of men, so his Confession begins. He knew he had been found by mercy. Can you begin your own story the same way?",
  query="Saint Patrick painting Tiepolo"),
 "03-18": dict(name="Saint Cyril of Jerusalem", dates="c. 313–386 · Jerusalem", match=["cyril"],
  history="Bishop of Jerusalem in the stormy years after the Council of Nicaea, three times driven into exile. His Catechetical Lectures, given to those preparing for baptism at Easter, are a priceless picture of how the early Church taught the Creed and the sacraments. Leo XIII named him a Doctor of the Church in 1883.",
  life="Cyril was probably born in or near Jerusalem and was ordained priest there. Around 350 he gave his famous lectures in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, built by Constantine over the place of the crucifixion and resurrection: a series on the Creed for those to be baptised at Easter, and a further set, which bear his name, on the mysteries of baptism, chrism and the Eucharist for the newly baptised. He became bishop soon after. Caught up in the Arian controversy and in a rivalry with Acacius of Caesarea, he was exiled three times, once on the charge of selling church treasures to feed the poor in a famine. In all he spent some sixteen years away from his see. He took part in the Council of Constantinople in 381, which confirmed the faith of Nicaea and his own orthodoxy, and died in Jerusalem in 386.",
  prayerTo="Saint Cyril, you taught the newly baptised to receive the Body of Christ as a king, with reverence and wonder. Pray for catechists and for all preparing to enter the Church this Easter. Help me to know the Creed I profess and to treasure the sacraments I receive. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for those to be baptised or received into the Church this Easter in your parish, and read the Creed slowly, one article at a time.",
  meditation="The lectures that bear his name teach the communicant to make the left hand a throne for the right, since it is to receive a King. How do you approach Holy Communion?",
  query="Saint Cyril of Jerusalem painting"),
 "03-19": dict(name="Saint Joseph", dates="First century · Nazareth", match=["joseph"],
  history="The husband of the Virgin Mary and foster father of Jesus, a just man of the house of David. The Gospels record no word of his, only his obedience: four times God spoke to him in dreams and four times he rose and did as he was told. Pius IX declared him Patron of the Universal Church in 1870.",
  life="Joseph was a craftsman of Nazareth, a tekton, which may mean a carpenter or a builder. Betrothed to Mary, he found she was with child and, being a just man, resolved to divorce her quietly, until an angel told him in a dream to take her as his wife. He took the family to Bethlehem for the census, fled with them by night into Egypt, and brought them home to Nazareth. With Mary he searched three days for the boy Jesus and found Him in the Temple. He is not mentioned again in the public ministry, and tradition holds that he died before it, in the arms of Jesus and Mary, which is why he is the patron of a happy death. Saint Teresa of Ávila spread devotion to him, Leo XIII wrote an encyclical on him, and John XXIII added his name to the Roman Canon.",
  prayerTo="Saint Joseph, guardian of the Redeemer and husband of Mary, you protected the Holy Family in silence and obedience. Protect my family and the whole Church. Teach me to work honestly, to listen for God’s voice and to act on it, and be with me at the hour of my death. Amen.",
  penance="This is a solemnity, so keep it as a feast even in Lent. In the Sicilian custom of Saint Joseph’s table, share a good meal with someone or give food to the poor.",
  meditation="Joseph says nothing in the Gospels. He rises and does what he is told. What has God already asked of you that you have not yet risen to do?",
  query="Saint Joseph with the Christ Child painting Guido Reni"),
 "04-07": dict(name="Saint John Baptist de La Salle", dates="1651–1719 · Reims and Rouen", match=["la salle"],
  history="A wealthy canon of Reims who gave up his position and his fortune to train teachers for the children of the poor. He founded the Brothers of the Christian Schools, taught in French rather than Latin, grouped pupils in classes and opened some of the first colleges for teachers. Pius XII named him patron of teachers.",
  life="Jean-Baptiste de La Salle was the eldest son of a noble family of Reims, a canon of the cathedral at sixteen and a priest at twenty-seven. In 1679 he met Adrien Nyel, who wanted to open free schools for poor boys, and helped him find teachers. Seeing how unprepared they were, he took them into his own house to live and pray with him, to the dismay of his family. He resigned his canonry and, in the famine of 1684, gave away his inheritance to the hungry, so that he would stand with his teachers in poverty. From them grew the Brothers of the Christian Schools, religious who were not priests and gave their lives to teaching. He met fierce opposition, lawsuits from the writing masters and conflict within his own institute, and went through periods of deep discouragement. He died at Rouen on Good Friday, 7 April 1719.",
  prayerTo="Saint John Baptist de La Salle, you gave up wealth and honour so that poor children might learn and know God. Pray for teachers, for all who form the young, and for children who have no one to teach them. Help me to remember that I stand always in the holy presence of God. Amen.",
  penance="Thank a teacher who formed you, or give to a school or charity that educates poor children. Make the Lasallian pause: stop several times today and remember you are in God’s presence.",
  meditation="Every lesson in his schools began with the words: let us remember that we are in the holy presence of God. Where do you most forget it?",
  query="Jean-Baptiste de La Salle portrait painting"),
 "04-11": dict(name="Saint Stanislaus", dates="c. 1030–1079 · Kraków", match=["stanislaus"],
  history="Bishop of Kraków who rebuked King Bolesław II for his cruelty and his sins, and was killed in 1079, by tradition at the king’s own hand while celebrating Mass. The facts of the quarrel are disputed by historians, but Poland has honoured him for centuries as a martyr for the moral law and as one of her patrons.",
  life="Stanislaus was born at Szczepanów in southern Poland, studied, perhaps at Gniezno, and was ordained priest. In 1072 he became bishop of Kraków. King Bolesław II, called the Bold, was a capable soldier and ruler, but the bishop came into conflict with him; the later chronicles speak of the king’s cruelty and immorality and of the bishop’s rebukes, ending in excommunication. Tradition holds that Bolesław, unable to find men willing to kill the bishop, struck him down himself in the church on the Skałka in Kraków. A popular legend tells that Stanislaus once raised a dead man, Piotrowin, to testify in a dispute over land. The king was driven into exile soon after. Stanislaus was canonised at Assisi in 1253, and his relics rest in Wawel Cathedral. John Paul II, his successor in Kraków, called him a patron of the moral order.",
  prayerTo="Saint Stanislaus, shepherd of Kraków, you spoke the truth to a king and paid for it with your life. Pray for bishops and for all who must correct the powerful. Give me courage to speak for what is right, gently and without fear, and to stand by the truth when it costs me. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for those in public authority, and for the Church in Poland. If you have been avoiding a necessary word of correction, ask for the grace to give it with charity.",
  meditation="He was killed, the tradition says, at the altar, in the middle of the Mass. Is there any truth you have kept quiet because speaking it would cost you?",
  query="Saint Stanislaus of Krakow painting"),
 "04-13": dict(name="Saint Martin I", dates="Died 655 · Rome, Constantinople and the Crimea", match=["martin i"],
  history="A pope who defended the truth that Christ has a human will as well as a divine one, against the policy of the Emperor. Arrested in Rome, tried in Constantinople and exiled to the Crimea, he died there of hunger and ill-treatment in 655. He is the last pope honoured as a martyr.",
  life="Martin, born at Todi in Umbria, was a deacon of Rome and the pope’s representative at Constantinople before his election in 649. The Emperor had forbidden all debate on whether Christ had one will or two, hoping to reconcile divided Christians with a formula that left His humanity incomplete. Within months of his election Martin held a synod at the Lateran, with Saint Maximus the Confessor’s support, which taught that Christ, true God and true man, has both a divine and a human will, and condemned the imperial decree. In 653 the Emperor Constans II had him seized in the Lateran, sick in bed, and shipped to Constantinople. There he was imprisoned, tried for treason, publicly stripped and condemned to death, a sentence changed to exile at Cherson in the Crimea. His letters from exile speak of hunger and of feeling forgotten. He died there in 655.",
  prayerTo="Saint Martin, you would not trade the truth about Christ for the favour of an emperor. Pray for the Pope and for all who suffer for the faith in prison or exile. When I feel forgotten, help me to trust that God forgets no one, and to stay faithful to the end. Amen.",
  penance="Martin died hungry and forsaken. Eat simply today and give the difference to a charity for prisoners or refugees, and pray for the Holy Father.",
  meditation="In exile he wrote that he was in God’s hands and prayed for those who had abandoned him. Whom do you find it hardest to pray for?",
  query="Pope Saint Martin I painting"),
 "04-14": dict(name="Saint Justin Martyr", dates="c. 100–165 · Samaria and Rome", match=["justin"], efMatch="Justin",
  history="A pagan philosopher from Samaria who found in Christianity the true philosophy he had been seeking. In Rome he taught openly as a Christian, wrote two Apologies defending the faith to the Emperor and gave the earliest full description of the Sunday Eucharist. He was beheaded around 165 with six companions. He is the patron of philosophers.",
  life="Justin was born at Flavia Neapolis, the modern Nablus, to a pagan family. In his Dialogue with Trypho he tells how he went from school to school, trying the Stoics, a Peripatetic, a Pythagorean and the Platonists, until an old man he met by the sea told him of the Hebrew prophets and of Christ, and ‘a fire was kindled in my soul’. After his conversion he kept the philosopher’s cloak and opened a school in Rome. His First Apology, addressed to Antoninus Pius, describes Christian worship: the gathering on the day of the sun, the readings from the prophets and the memoirs of the apostles, the prayers, the bread and wine over which thanks are given, and the deacons taking it to those absent. He taught that seeds of the Word were scattered in every people. Denounced as a Christian, he was tried before the prefect Rusticus and refused to sacrifice.",
  prayerTo="Saint Justin, you searched for wisdom until you found it in Christ, the Word made flesh. Pray for all who seek the truth, for students, teachers and thinkers, and for me, that I may give a reason for the hope that is in me, with gentleness and courage. Amen.",
  penance="Spend some time today learning your faith: read a chapter of the Catechism or a page of the early Fathers. Pray for a friend who is searching for meaning.",
  meditation="A fire was kindled in my soul, and a love of the prophets and of those who are friends of Christ took hold of me. What first kindled that fire in you?",
  query="Saint Justin Martyr painting"),
 "04-21": dict(name="Saint Anselm", dates="1033–1109 · Aosta, Bec and Canterbury", match=["anselm"], efMatch="Anselm",
  history="An Italian who became abbot of Bec in Normandy and then Archbishop of Canterbury, Anselm was the greatest theologian of his age. He described his work as faith seeking understanding, and wrote the Proslogion and Why God Became Man. Twice exiled for defending the freedom of the Church, he was named a Doctor of the Church in 1720.",
  life="Born at Aosta, Anselm left home after a quarrel with his father and crossed the Alps to the abbey of Bec, drawn by the fame of its prior, Lanfranc. He became a monk there in 1060, then prior and abbot, and was loved as a gentle and patient teacher. At Bec he wrote the Monologion and the Proslogion, in which he speaks of God as that than which nothing greater can be thought. In 1093, during a visit to England, he was made Archbishop of Canterbury against his will, the crozier pressed into his closed hand. He clashed with William Rufus and then Henry I over the rights of the Church, and spent years in exile. At the Council of Bari in 1098 he defended the Western teaching on the Holy Spirit. In exile he finished Cur Deus Homo, on why God became man. He died at Canterbury on 21 April 1109.",
  prayerTo="Saint Anselm, you sought to understand what you believed, and to love what you understood. Pray for theologians, for students and for the Church in England. Teach me to seek God with my mind and my heart, and to find, in seeking Him, that He has already found me. Amen.",
  penance="Give a quarter of an hour today to thinking about God in silence: sit with one line of the Creed and ask Him to show you more.",
  meditation="I do not seek to understand in order to believe, he wrote, but I believe in order to understand. What do you believe that you would like to understand better?",
  query="Anselm of Canterbury painting"),
 "04-22": dict(name="Saints Soter and Caius", dates="Second and third centuries · Rome", match=["soter"], efMatch="Soter",
  history="Two early popes kept together on one day. Soter led the Roman Church around 166 to 175 and was known for its generosity to poorer churches. Caius governed it from 283 to 296, in the years of peace before the great persecution of Diocletian, and was buried in the catacomb of Callixtus. Both are honoured in tradition as martyrs.",
  life="Little is known of either pope, and the Church honours them for what is certain. Eusebius preserves part of a letter from Dionysius, bishop of Corinth, thanking Soter and the Roman Christians for the alms they sent to churches in need and to Christians condemned to the mines, a custom Dionysius says Rome had kept from the beginning; he adds that Soter wrote to Corinth like a father to his children. Caius, according to the Liber Pontificalis, came from Dalmatia, and later legend made him a relative of the Emperor Diocletian. His tomb was found in the catacomb of Callixtus in the nineteenth century, with part of his epitaph. Whether either man died a martyr is not known for certain; the old calendar honours them as such, and both lived and led their flock when to be a Christian, still more its bishop, was to live under threat.",
  prayerTo="Saints Soter and Caius, shepherds of the Church of Rome, you cared for poor and distant churches and kept the faith in dangerous times. Pray for the Pope and the bishops, for Christians in need throughout the world, and for me, that I may be generous to those I will never meet. Amen.",
  penance="Soter’s Church sent alms to Christians far away. Give today to a charity that supports persecuted or poor Christian communities abroad.",
  meditation="Dionysius thanked Soter for writing to them as a loving father writes to his children. Who needs a letter from you?",
  query="Pope Soter Sistine Chapel"),
 "04-23": dict(name="Saint George", dates="Died c. 303 · Lydda, Palestine", match=["george"],
  history="A soldier martyred at Lydda in Palestine, probably under Diocletian, and venerated across East and West from the earliest centuries. The story of the dragon came much later and is legend. Crusaders brought his cult home, Edward III placed the Order of the Garter under his protection, and he became the patron saint of England.",
  life="Almost nothing is known for certain of George’s life. His tomb at Lydda, the modern Lod, was a place of pilgrimage by the sixth century, and churches were dedicated to him throughout the Christian East. Pope Gelasius in 494 counted him among those whose names are rightly honoured but whose deeds are known only to God. The legend of the dragon, slain to save a princess and a town that then accepted baptism, was spread through the West by the Golden Legend in the thirteenth century. English devotion grew with the Crusades; his feast was kept in England from 1222, Edward III founded the Order of the Garter in his honour around 1348, and his red cross became the national flag. He is also a patron of soldiers, scouts, Georgia, Catalonia and Portugal, and Eastern Christians call him the Great Martyr.",
  prayerTo="Saint George, soldier of Christ, you gave up rank and life rather than deny your Lord. Pray for England, for soldiers and all who serve and protect others, and for me, that I may fight the dragons of my own sins with courage and trust in the grace of God. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for England and for those who serve in the armed forces. Choose one fault you have been tolerating and take it on with a small, definite act.",
  meditation="The dragon is legend; the courage is not. What is the dragon in your own life that you have been afraid to face?",
  query="Saint George and the Dragon Paolo Uccello"),
 "04-24": dict(name="Saint Fidelis of Sigmaringen", dates="1578–1622 · Swabia and the Grisons", match=["fidelis"], efMatch="Fidelis",
  history="A lawyer known as the poor man’s advocate who left the courts to become a Capuchin friar. Sent to preach in the Swiss canton of the Grisons during years of bitter religious and political conflict, he was killed there in 1622. He was the first martyr of the Congregation for the Propagation of the Faith.",
  life="Mark Roy was born at Sigmaringen in Swabia and took a doctorate in law at Freiburg. He spent some years travelling through Europe as tutor to young noblemen, and then practised law at Ensisheim in Alsace, where he took the cases of the poor without fee. Disgusted by the dishonesty of his profession, he entered the Capuchins in 1612, and was given the name Fidelis, from the promise in the Apocalypse: be faithful unto death, and I will give you the crown of life. He served as guardian of several friaries and nursed soldiers through an epidemic. In 1622 the new Congregation de Propaganda Fide sent him to preach in the Prättigau valley, then caught in war between Austrian forces and local Protestant communities. On 24 April, after preaching at Seewis, he was attacked by armed men on the road and killed. He was canonised in 1746.",
  prayerTo="Saint Fidelis, you defended the poor in the courts and preached Christ in a land divided by war. Pray for lawyers and judges, for missionaries, and for peace between Christians. Help me to be faithful unto death, to speak the truth in love, and to forgive those who oppose me. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for Christian unity today, and for someone whose beliefs differ from yours. If you can, give to a legal aid charity in honour of the poor man’s lawyer.",
  meditation="Be faithful unto death, and I will give you the crown of life. In what small thing is God asking you to be faithful today?",
  query="Fidelis of Sigmaringen Tiepolo"),
 "04-25": dict(name="Saint Mark the Evangelist", dates="First century · Jerusalem, Rome and Alexandria", match=["mark"],
  history="John Mark, the companion of Paul, Barnabas and Peter, wrote the shortest of the four Gospels, probably the first. Early tradition says he set down the preaching of Peter in Rome. He is held to have founded the Church of Alexandria, and his relics were carried to Venice in 828. His symbol is the lion.",
  life="The Acts of the Apostles tells us that the first Christians of Jerusalem met in the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, and that Peter went there when an angel freed him from prison. Mark set out with his cousin Barnabas and with Paul on their first journey, but left them at Perga, and Paul refused to take him again, so that Barnabas and Paul parted. Years later Paul asked for him, ‘for he is useful to me for ministry’, and Peter calls him ‘my son’. Papias, writing early in the second century, says that Mark was Peter’s interpreter and wrote down accurately what Peter remembered of the Lord. His Gospel is vivid and urgent, full of the word ‘immediately’, and shows the disciples’ failures plainly. Some think he was the young man who fled naked from Gethsemane. Tradition says he was martyred at Alexandria.",
  prayerTo="Saint Mark, you once turned back from the work, and by God’s grace became useful for His service and wrote the Good News of Jesus Christ. Pray for all who have failed and been given a second chance, and for me, that I may follow Christ without turning back. Amen.",
  penance="Read the Gospel of Mark today, or as much of it as you can; it takes about two hours to read aloud. Be reconciled with someone you have fallen out with.",
  meditation="Paul once refused to take him; later he called him useful. Is there someone you have written off whom God has not?",
  query="Saint Mark the Evangelist painting Mantegna"),
 "04-26": dict(name="Saints Cletus and Marcellinus", dates="First and fourth centuries · Rome", match=["cletus"], efMatch="Cletus",
  history="Two popes kept on one day. Cletus, also called Anacletus, was the third bishop of Rome after Peter and Linus, and is named in the Roman Canon. Marcellinus led the Roman Church from 296 to 304, as the great persecution under Diocletian broke out, and died in or soon after it. Both are honoured by tradition as martyrs.",
  life="Saint Irenaeus, writing around 180, lists the bishops of Rome from the apostles: Linus, then Anacletus, then Clement. That is almost all we know of Cletus, but it is a great deal: he stood in the line of those who kept the faith handed on by Peter and Paul, and the Church names him every day in the Roman Canon. Marcellinus became pope in 296. In 303 Diocletian ordered churches destroyed, the Scriptures burned and Christians compelled to sacrifice. Later sources say that Marcellinus gave way under pressure and offered incense, then repented and died a martyr; others say nothing of a lapse, and historians are uncertain. He was buried in the catacomb of Priscilla. Whatever the truth, his story as the Church has told it is one of weakness and mercy, and of a shepherd who came back.",
  prayerTo="Saints Cletus and Marcellinus, shepherds of Rome in its first centuries, you handed on the faith of the apostles in times of danger. Pray for the Pope and for all who are tempted to give way under pressure. If I fall, help me to rise again quickly and return to Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Make a good examination of conscience today and, if you can, go to Confession. Pray for any Christian who has given up the faith under pressure.",
  meditation="The Church names Cletus every day in the Roman Canon, and we know almost nothing else about him. Can you be content to be faithful and unknown?",
  query="Pope Cletus Sistine Chapel"),
 "04-27": dict(name="Saint Peter Canisius", dates="1521–1597 · Nijmegen, Germany and Fribourg", match=["canisius"], efMatch="Canisius",
  history="The first Dutch Jesuit, who spent fifty years renewing the Catholic faith in Germany, Austria and Switzerland after the upheaval of the Reformation. He founded colleges, preached tirelessly and wrote catechisms that went through hundreds of editions. He is called the second apostle of Germany and was named a Doctor of the Church in 1925.",
  life="Peter Kanis was born at Nijmegen and studied at Cologne. In 1543 he made the Spiritual Exercises under Saint Peter Faber at Mainz and entered the young Society of Jesus. He attended the Council of Trent as a theologian, taught at Ingolstadt and Vienna, and became the first provincial of the Jesuits in Upper Germany. He founded or helped found colleges at Prague, Munich, Innsbruck and elsewhere, preached constantly and refused the archbishopric of Vienna. His catechisms, beginning in 1555, were so widely used that in some German lands a catechism was simply called a ‘Canisius’. He urged that those who had left the Church be treated with patience and charity, and he relied more on teaching and example than on argument. His last years were spent at Fribourg in Switzerland, where he founded the college of Saint Michael and died in 1597.",
  prayerTo="Saint Peter Canisius, you rebuilt the Church in your time by patient teaching and tireless love. Pray for catechists, priests and teachers, and for all who have drifted from the faith. Give me a clear knowledge of what the Church believes, and the charity to share it gently. Amen.",
  penance="Learn one answer from the Catechism by heart today. Pray for someone who has left the Church, and treat them with special kindness.",
  meditation="He spent fifty years teaching the faith to children and simple people, one question and answer at a time. Could you explain your faith simply if a child asked you?",
  query="Peter Canisius portrait painting"),
 "04-28": dict(name="Saint Louis Grignion de Montfort", dates="1673–1716 · Brittany and Poitou", match=["montfort"],
  history="A Breton priest who walked the roads of western France preaching missions to the poor, singing hymns he wrote himself and setting up great calvaries. Often opposed and expelled, he founded the Company of Mary and, with Blessed Marie-Louise Trichet, the Daughters of Wisdom. His True Devotion to Mary taught total consecration to Jesus through Mary.",
  life="Louis-Marie Grignion was born at Montfort-sur-Meu in Brittany, studied at Saint-Sulpice in Paris and was ordained in 1700. As chaplain of the hospital at Poitiers he met Marie-Louise Trichet, with whom he founded the Daughters of Wisdom to care for the sick and poor. His blunt preaching and his austerity made enemies, and he was more than once forbidden to preach in a diocese. In 1706 he walked to Rome, and Pope Clement XI named him missionary apostolic. Back in France he preached some two hundred missions, mostly in country parishes, restored churches, wrote hundreds of hymns and built a huge calvary at Pontchâteau, which the authorities ordered pulled down. His treatise on True Devotion lay hidden until 1842 and has since shaped millions, among them John Paul II, whose motto Totus Tuus came from it. He died at Saint-Laurent-sur-Sèvre in 1716, aged forty-three.",
  prayerTo="Saint Louis de Montfort, you gave yourself wholly to Jesus through Mary and spent your life for the poor. Pray for missionaries and for all who preach the Gospel to the forgotten. Teach me to belong entirely to Christ through His Mother, and to bear opposition with patience and joy. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Rosary today, or one decade if time is short, and give it to Our Lady for her to use as she wills. Do a kindness for someone who is poor or sick.",
  meditation="Totus tuus: I am all yours. Is there any part of your life you have kept back from Christ?",
  query="Louis de Montfort painting"),
 "04-29": dict(name="Saint Catherine of Siena", dates="1347–1380 · Siena, Avignon and Rome", match=["catherine"],
  history="A dyer’s daughter of Siena, a Dominican tertiary who never learned to write well, yet counselled popes and princes, made peace between cities and urged the Pope to return from Avignon to Rome. Her Dialogue and her letters make her one of the great teachers of the Church. She is a Doctor of the Church and a patron of Europe and Italy.",
  life="Caterina Benincasa was one of the youngest of more than twenty children of a dyer and his wife Lapa. As a girl she gave herself to Christ and resisted every plan for her marriage. At about sixteen she joined the Mantellate, Dominican laywomen who lived at home, and spent three years in near silence in a small room of her father’s house. Then she went out to nurse the sick and serve the poor, above all in the plague of 1374, and a circle of disciples gathered round her, among them Blessed Raymond of Capua, her confessor. She received the stigmata at Pisa in 1375, visible to no one. In 1376 she went to Avignon and urged Gregory XI to return to Rome, which he did. When the Great Schism broke out she supported Urban VI and spent herself praying for the unity of the Church. She died in Rome, aged thirty-three.",
  prayerTo="Saint Catherine, you loved Christ crucified and His Church with a fire that the whole world felt. Pray for the Pope, for the unity and renewal of the Church, and for Europe. Help me to dwell in the cell of self-knowledge, and to speak the truth with courage and love. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the Pope and for the unity of the Church. Catherine fasted hard, but she also nursed the sick: visit or help someone who is ill.",
  meditation="She taught that we must build a cell of self-knowledge within the soul, and there find both our own nothingness and the goodness of God. Where is your inner cell?",
  query="Saint Catherine of Siena receiving the stigmata Beccafumi"),
 "04-30": dict(name="Saint Pius V", dates="1504–1572 · Lombardy and Rome", match=["pius"],
  history="A Dominican friar from a poor family who became pope in 1566 and put the reforms of the Council of Trent into effect. He issued the Roman Catechism, the reformed Breviary and the Missal of 1570, and lived as austerely as a friar. He called Christendom to pray the Rosary before the victory of Lepanto in 1571.",
  life="Antonio Ghislieri was born at Bosco, near Alessandria, and kept sheep as a boy. He entered the Dominicans at fourteen, taking the name Michele, and taught theology for years. He served as an inquisitor, then as bishop and cardinal, and was elected pope in 1566. He set about carrying out the decrees of Trent: he published the Roman Catechism, reformed the Breviary and the Missal, insisted that bishops live in their dioceses, cleared the papal court of luxury and named Saint Thomas Aquinas a Doctor of the Church. He went on wearing his white Dominican habit, from which, tradition says, comes the white cassock of the popes. He visited hospitals and washed the feet of the poor. Facing the advance of the Ottoman fleet, he formed the Holy League and asked all Christendom to pray the Rosary. He died on 1 May 1572 and was canonised in 1712.",
  prayerTo="Saint Pius, shepherd and reformer, you lived simply in the midst of great power and gave the Church the treasures of her worship. Pray for the Pope and the bishops, for the reform of the Church in every age, and for me, that I may begin with the reform of my own life. Amen.",
  penance="Pray a decade of the Rosary today for peace in the world. Pius lived on simple food: keep your meals plain today and give something to the poor.",
  meditation="He kept the habit of a friar on the throne of Peter. What habit of simplicity could you keep in the midst of your own comforts?",
  query="Pope Pius V portrait Passarotti"),
 "05-01": dict(name="Saint Joseph the Worker", dates="Instituted 1955", match=["worker", "workman"], efMatch="Joseph",
  history="Pius XII instituted this feast in 1955 to give May Day, the festival of labour, a Christian meaning. Jesus was known in Nazareth as the carpenter’s son, and Joseph shows the dignity of ordinary work done honestly for family and neighbour. The day is a reminder of the Church’s teaching on the rights of workers.",
  life="The Gospels call Joseph a tekton, a craftsman, and Jesus Himself was known as ‘the carpenter’. For some thirty years the Son of God lived a hidden life of work in a small town, learning a trade from His foster father. The Church has always seen in this a sign that work is not a curse but a share in God’s own creating. In 1891 Leo XIII, in Rerum Novarum, defended the right of workers to a just wage and to form associations, and later popes developed his teaching. When Pius XII addressed Italian workers on 1 May 1955 he gave them this feast, so that the day already kept by working people around the world would also honour the patron of workers. John Paul II, who had himself worked in a quarry and a factory, wrote in Laborem Exercens that through work man becomes more a man.",
  prayerTo="Saint Joseph the Worker, you earned your bread by the work of your hands and taught the Son of God your trade. Pray for all who work and all who cannot find work, for employers and those they employ. Help me to do my work well, honestly and for the glory of God. Amen.",
  penance="Do your ordinary work today with special care, offering it to God. Thank someone whose work serves you, and pray for those who are unemployed or underpaid.",
  meditation="Is not this the carpenter? The townspeople meant it as a dismissal. How might your own ordinary work become a place of holiness?",
  query="Saint Joseph the Carpenter Georges de La Tour"),
 "05-02": dict(name="Saint Athanasius", dates="c. 296–373 · Alexandria", match=["athanasius"],
  history="Bishop of Alexandria for forty-five years, Athanasius was the great defender of the faith of Nicaea, that the Son is true God, of one substance with the Father. Exiled five times by four emperors, he never gave way. He wrote On the Incarnation and the Life of Antony, and is a Doctor of the Church.",
  life="As a young deacon Athanasius went with his bishop Alexander to the Council of Nicaea in 325, which condemned Arius’s teaching that the Son was a creature. Three years later he succeeded Alexander as bishop. The Arian party, often supported by the imperial court, worked to remove him, and he was banished five times, to Trier in Gaul, to Rome and, more than once, to hiding among the monks of the Egyptian desert. In all he spent about seventeen years in exile. His short book On the Incarnation explains why the Word became flesh: to restore the image of God in us and to overcome death. His Life of Antony spread the ideal of monastic life through the West. In his Easter letter of 367 he listed the twenty-seven books of the New Testament as we have them. He died in Alexandria in 373, at peace with his flock.",
  prayerTo="Saint Athanasius, you stood for the truth that Jesus Christ is God, though emperors and bishops turned against you. Pray for the Church in every age of confusion, and for me, that I may hold fast to the faith, worship the Son as true God, and not fear standing alone. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Nicene Creed slowly today, bowing at the words of the Incarnation. Pray for bishops who are under pressure to bend the truth.",
  meditation="He became man so that we might be made god. What does it mean for you that God wishes to share His own life with you?",
  query="Saint Athanasius painting"),
 "05-03": dict(name="Saints Philip and James", dates="Apostles and martyrs", match=["philip"],
  history="Two of the Twelve, honoured together since their relics were placed in the Church of the Holy Apostles in Rome. Philip of Bethsaida brought Nathanael to Jesus and asked Him at the Last Supper to show them the Father. James, son of Alphaeus, is identified in the Western tradition with James, the brother of the Lord and bishop of Jerusalem.",
  life="Saint John’s Gospel gives us Philip in a few sharp moments. Called by Jesus, he went at once to Nathanael: come and see. Before the feeding of the five thousand Jesus tested him by asking where to buy bread, and Philip could only count the cost. When some Greeks wished to see Jesus, they came to Philip. At the Last Supper he said, ‘Lord, show us the Father,’ and heard the answer: whoever has seen me has seen the Father. Tradition says he preached in Phrygia and died at Hierapolis. James, son of Alphaeus, is called James the Less. The Latin Church has long identified him with James the brother of the Lord, who led the Church in Jerusalem, spoke at the Council of Jerusalem and, according to Josephus, was stoned around 62. Their relics were enshrined in Rome in the sixth century.",
  prayerTo="Saints Philip and James, apostles of the Lord, you saw the face of the Father in Jesus and gave your lives for Him. Pray for the Church, for those who seek God, and for me, that I may see Christ more clearly, and that my faith may show itself in works. Amen.",
  penance="James wrote that faith without works is dead. Do one practical work of mercy today, and bring someone, as Philip did, to come and see.",
  meditation="Lord, show us the Father, and we shall be satisfied. Have I been so long with you, and yet you do not know me? Where have you missed Him?",
  query="Saint Philip apostle painting Rubens"),
 "05-04": dict(name="Saint Monica", dates="c. 332–387 · Thagaste and Ostia", match=["monica"], efMatch="Monica",
  history="The mother of Saint Augustine, who prayed and wept for her son through years of unbelief and restless living until she saw him baptised. She also won her hot-tempered pagan husband to Christ by patience. Augustine’s Confessions give her portrait. She is the patron of mothers and of all who wait for a loved one’s conversion.",
  life="Monica was a Christian of Thagaste in North Africa, married young to Patricius, a pagan official of uncertain temper and loose morals, and living with a difficult mother-in-law. By patience and kindness she won them both, and Patricius was baptised before his death. Her son Augustine was brilliant and restless, took a mistress, and joined the Manichees. Monica followed him with prayers and tears. A bishop, tired of her pleading, told her: it is not possible that the son of these tears should perish. When Augustine slipped away to Rome she followed him, and then to Milan, where Saint Ambrose became his teacher. At Easter 387 Ambrose baptised him. On the journey home, at Ostia, mother and son spoke together at a window of the life of heaven. A few days later she fell ill, and asked only that they remember her at the altar of the Lord. She died there, aged fifty-six.",
  prayerTo="Saint Monica, you never stopped praying for your son, and God answered beyond all you asked. Pray for mothers and fathers, for those who have left the faith, and for me in my waiting. Give me your patience, your perseverance and your trust that no tear offered to God is wasted. Amen.",
  penance="Offer a small fast today, perhaps one meal kept very simple, for someone you love who has wandered from God. Pray for them by name.",
  meditation="Lay this body anywhere, she said. Only this I ask: that you remember me at the altar of the Lord. Whom will you remember at Mass?",
  query="Saints Augustine and Monica Ary Scheffer"),
 "05-10": dict(name="Saint Antoninus", dates="1389–1459 · Florence", match=["antoninus"], efMatch="Antoninus",
  history="A Dominican friar of Florence who became its archbishop and lived as poor as his flock. He helped found the friary of San Marco, gave away almost everything he had, set up a confraternity to help families ashamed to beg, and wrote a great work of moral theology that dealt with trade, money and the just price.",
  life="Antonino Pierozzi, son of a Florentine notary, entered the Dominicans at Fiesole as a teenager, under the influence of Blessed John Dominici and his strict reform. He was prior of several friaries, and with the help of Cosimo de’ Medici founded San Marco in Florence, where Fra Angelico painted the cells. He served as a theologian at the Council of Florence. In 1446 Pope Eugene IV named him archbishop of Florence, and he accepted only under obedience. His household was tiny and his clothes poor; he sold his own goods in famine and plague, visited his parishes on foot, and was known for settling disputes. He founded the Buonomini di San Martino to help the ‘shamefaced poor’, families fallen into need who were too proud to beg; it still exists. His Summa of moral theology was one of the first to treat economic life seriously. He died in 1459.",
  prayerTo="Saint Antoninus, you governed your city as a shepherd and lived as a poor friar. Pray for Florence, for bishops and civic leaders, and for those who work in trade and finance. Help me to be honest in all my dealings and generous to those who are ashamed to ask for help. Amen.",
  penance="Help someone quietly today who would be embarrassed to ask, without letting them know it came from you. Review one of your financial habits in the light of justice.",
  meditation="He cared especially for the poor who hid their poverty. Who around you is struggling in silence?",
  query="Saint Antoninus of Florence painting"),
 "05-11": dict(name="Saints Philip and James", dates="Apostles and martyrs", match=["philip"], efMatch="Philip",
  history="The 1962 calendar keeps the apostles Philip and James today, the date to which their feast was moved in 1955 to make way for Saint Joseph the Worker. Philip brought others to meet Jesus; James, called the Less, is honoured in the West as the brother of the Lord, first bishop of Jerusalem and author of the Letter of James.",
  life="Philip was from Bethsaida, the town of Andrew and Peter. He is the apostle who says ‘come and see’, who is puzzled by the hungry crowd, who brings the Greeks to Jesus, and who at the Last Supper hears Christ say, ‘Philip, he who sees me sees the Father.’ The Letter of James, which the Western tradition has attributed to this James, is the most practical book of the New Testament: be doers of the word and not hearers only; care for orphans and widows; control the tongue; do not favour the rich; pray for the sick and anoint them, a passage the Church reads of the sacrament of anointing. Tradition holds that James was put to death in Jerusalem around 62, and that Philip died at Hierapolis in Asia Minor. Their feast was kept on 1 May for centuries, from the dedication of their church in Rome.",
  prayerTo="Holy apostles Philip and James, you followed the Lord and gave your lives as witnesses to Him. Pray that I may be a doer of the word and not a hearer only, that I may guard my tongue, and that I may bring others to come and see the Lord Jesus. Amen.",
  penance="Read the Letter of James today, five short chapters, and put one of its commands into practice: guard your tongue, or help a widow or someone alone.",
  meditation="Be doers of the word, and not hearers only. Which word of Christ have you heard many times and never done?",
  query="Saint James the Less painting El Greco"),
 "05-12": dict(name="Saints Nereus, Achilleus and Pancras", dates="Early martyrs · Rome", match=["nereus", "pancras"], efMatch="Nereus",
  history="Three early Roman martyrs. Nereus and Achilleus were soldiers who, Pope Damasus recorded in verse, threw down their arms when they came to believe, and died for Christ. Pancras was a boy of about fourteen beheaded on the Via Aurelia, probably under Diocletian. His cult reached England early, and churches in Canterbury and London bear his name.",
  life="Nereus and Achilleus were buried in the catacomb of Domitilla on the Via Ardeatina, where a basilica was later built over their tomb. Pope Damasus, in the fourth century, wrote an inscription for them: they had served a tyrant’s cruel orders as soldiers, then, suddenly converted, they fled the camp, threw away their shields and weapons, and rejoiced to bear witness to Christ. A later legend linked them with Flavia Domitilla, a Christian of the imperial family. Of Pancras we know the place of his burial on the Via Aurelia and the great devotion paid him; tradition says he was an orphan from Phrygia brought to Rome by his uncle, and that he was beheaded at about fourteen. Gregory of Tours wrote that false oaths sworn at his tomb were punished. Pope Gregory the Great sent relics of Pancras to England, and a church in his honour stood in Canterbury.",
  prayerTo="Saints Nereus and Achilleus, you laid down your weapons for Christ; Saint Pancras, you gave your young life for Him. Pray for soldiers, for children and young people, and for all who must choose between the faith and the demands of the world. Give me courage to be faithful in small things and in great. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for young people who are under pressure to abandon their faith. Keep your word today in everything, however small, in honour of Pancras, patron of oaths.",
  meditation="They threw away their shields and rejoiced to carry the triumph of Christ. What defences would you have to lay down to follow Him more freely?",
  query="Saint Pancras painting"),
 "05-13": dict(name="Our Lady of Fatima", dates="Apparitions of 1917", match=["fatima"],
  history="From 13 May to 13 October 1917, Our Lady appeared six times to three shepherd children at the Cova da Iria near Fátima in Portugal, asking for prayer, penance and the daily Rosary. The apparitions were approved by the local bishop in 1930. Two of the children, Francisco and Jacinta Marto, were canonised in 2017.",
  life="Lúcia dos Santos, aged ten, and her cousins Francisco and Jacinta Marto, nine and seven, were minding sheep when, they said, a lady brighter than the sun appeared above a holm oak. She asked them to return on the thirteenth of each month, to pray the Rosary every day for peace and the end of the war, and to offer sacrifices for sinners. The children suffered disbelief, threats and even a short imprisonment. In July they were shown a vision of hell and given a message asking for devotion to the Immaculate Heart of Mary. On 13 October a large crowd, tens of thousands strong, reported seeing the sun dance in the sky. Francisco and Jacinta died young in the influenza epidemic; Lúcia became a Carmelite and died in 2005. John Paul II, shot on 13 May 1981, believed Our Lady had saved his life. As with all approved apparitions, belief in them is not required, but they are a trustworthy call to the Gospel.",
  prayerTo="Our Lady of Fatima, you asked three children to pray and to make sacrifices for sinners, and they answered with generous hearts. Teach me to pray the Rosary faithfully, to offer my small sufferings for others, and to take refuge in your Immaculate Heart. Pray for peace in the world. Amen.",
  prayer="O my Jesus, forgive us our sins, save us from the fire of hell; lead all souls to heaven, especially those most in need of thy mercy.",
  prayerSource="The Fatima prayer, taught by Our Lady in 1917, said after each decade of the Rosary",
  penance="Pray the Rosary today for peace, and make one small sacrifice for sinners, as the children did: a drink of water given up, or a complaint left unsaid.",
  meditation="The children were asked to offer small sacrifices for others. What small, hidden sacrifice could you make today for someone far from God?",
  query="Our Lady of Fatima 1917 Cova da Iria"),
 "05-14": dict(name="Saint Matthias", dates="First century · Judea", match=["matthias"],
  history="The disciple chosen by lot to take the place of Judas among the Twelve, after the Ascension and before Pentecost. He had followed Jesus from the baptism of John to the day He was taken up, and so could be a witness to the resurrection. Scripture says nothing more of him. He is the patron of reformed lives.",
  life="In the first chapter of Acts, Peter stands up among some hundred and twenty disciples and says that one must be chosen to replace Judas, a man who had been with them all the time the Lord Jesus went in and out among them. Two were put forward, Joseph called Barsabbas and Matthias. The apostles prayed, ‘Lord, you know the hearts of all men,’ and cast lots, and the lot fell on Matthias. That is all the New Testament tells us. Later traditions say he preached in Judea and further afield, in Cappadocia or Ethiopia, and died a martyr. Relics said to be his were brought to Trier, where the abbey of Saint Matthias claims the only tomb of an apostle north of the Alps. He was chosen for one task: to be a witness to the resurrection. He took the place left by a betrayal.",
  prayerTo="Saint Matthias, you were chosen to fill the place of one who betrayed the Lord, and you bore faithful witness to His resurrection. Pray for all who are called to new responsibilities, for those discerning their vocation, and for me, that I may accept whatever task God gives me. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for someone who has betrayed or abandoned the faith, and for vocations to the priesthood. Take on a duty someone else has dropped.",
  meditation="Lord, you know the hearts of all. Matthias was not the obvious choice; God chose him. Are you willing to be chosen for what you did not seek?",
  query="Saint Matthias apostle painting"),
 "05-18": dict(name="Saint John I", dates="Died 526 · Tuscany, Constantinople and Ravenna", match=["john i"],
  history="A pope sent against his will by Theodoric, the Arian Gothic king of Italy, to plead with the Emperor in Constantinople on behalf of the Arians. He was the first pope to visit Constantinople and was received with great honour. When he returned, Theodoric had him imprisoned at Ravenna, where he died. He is honoured as a martyr.",
  life="John was a Tuscan, an elderly deacon of the Roman Church when he was elected pope in 523. Italy was ruled by Theodoric, king of the Ostrogoths, an Arian Christian who had governed tolerantly for many years but grew suspicious as he aged; he had the philosopher Boethius put to death around this time. When the Emperor Justin I began to take churches from the Arians in the East, Theodoric forced the pope to lead an embassy to Constantinople to have the measures reversed. John was greeted there by the Emperor and the people, and celebrated Easter in Hagia Sophia in the Latin rite. The mission was only partly successful, and on his return Theodoric held him at Ravenna, where he died in May 526, worn out by his treatment. His body was carried to Rome and buried in Saint Peter’s.",
  prayerTo="Saint John, you were sent on an impossible errand and suffered for refusing to betray your flock. Pray for the Pope, for Christians who live under hostile powers, and for unity between East and West. When I am placed in a hard position, help me to act with honesty and trust in God. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the unity of Christians of East and West, and for anyone you know who is caught between conflicting loyalties.",
  meditation="He was asked to do what he could not in conscience fully do, and he went anyway, as far as he could go. Where must you obey without compromising the truth?",
  query="Pope John I painting"),
 "05-20": dict(name="Saint Bernardine of Siena", dates="1380–1444 · Siena and L’Aquila", match=["bernardine"],
  history="A Franciscan friar and the most famous preacher of fifteenth-century Italy, who crossed the country on foot drawing vast crowds. He spread devotion to the Holy Name of Jesus, showing a tablet with the letters IHS surrounded by rays of the sun. He refused three bishoprics and helped renew the Observant Franciscans.",
  life="Bernardino degli Albizzeschi was born at Massa Marittima, orphaned young and raised by devout aunts in Siena. When plague struck the city in 1400 he and a group of young men ran the great hospital of Santa Maria della Scala for months, and he nearly died. In 1402 he joined the Franciscans of the strict Observance. After years of quiet he began to preach, and for the rest of his life travelled Italy, preaching for hours in the open air because no church could hold the crowds. He spoke plainly about gambling, usury, vanity and the feuds that divided Italian towns, and people burned dice, cards and ornaments in the squares. Some accused his devotion to the Holy Name of novelty, but Pope Martin V cleared him. As vicar general he helped the Observants grow from a few hundred friars to thousands. He died at L’Aquila and was canonised in 1450.",
  prayerTo="Saint Bernardine, you preached the Holy Name of Jesus to the crowds and turned many from vanity and hatred. Pray for preachers and for all who speak in public. Teach me to honour the name of Jesus, to use my words to heal and not to wound, and to make peace. Amen.",
  penance="Make reparation today for the misuse of the name of Jesus: say it reverently, bow your head at it, and avoid all careless or harsh speech.",
  meditation="He held up the name of Jesus, carved and gilded, so that people would love it. How do you speak His name?",
  query="Saint Bernardino of Siena preaching Sano di Pietro"),
 "05-21": dict(name="Saint Christopher Magallanes and Companions", dates="Martyred 1915–1937 · Mexico", match=["magallanes"],
  history="Twenty-five Mexican martyrs, twenty-two priests and three laymen, killed in the years when the public practice of the faith was restricted and priests were hunted, above all during the Cristero War of 1926 to 1929. Christopher Magallanes, a parish priest of Jalisco, was shot in 1927 after forgiving his executioners. John Paul II canonised them in 2000.",
  life="Cristóbal Magallanes Jara was born in 1869 at Totatiche in Jalisco and worked the land before entering the seminary. As parish priest of his home town he built schools, founded a seminary, and cared for the poor and for the native Huichol people. When the laws of the 1920s closed churches and forbade priests to minister, he kept serving his people in secret and reopened his seminary in hiding. He wrote against taking up arms, though many Catholics had joined the Cristero rising. On 21 May 1927 he was arrested on his way to celebrate Mass, and four days later he was shot at Colotlán without trial, together with his young assistant, Saint Agustín Caloca. He gave away his last possessions to the soldiers, absolved them, and prayed that his blood might bring peace to Mexico. His companions were priests and laymen from several dioceses who died in similar ways.",
  prayerTo="Saint Christopher Magallanes and holy martyrs of Mexico, you served Christ’s people when it was forbidden and forgave those who killed you. Pray for the Church in Mexico and wherever priests are persecuted. Give me courage to keep the faith in hidden ways when I cannot keep it openly, and a heart that forgives. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for priests who serve in danger, and for persecutors. Forgive someone today, in your heart at least, as the martyrs forgave their executioners.",
  meditation="He gave his executioners his blessing and what little he had. What would it take for you to bless someone who has harmed you?",
  query="Cristóbal Magallanes Jara"),
 "05-22": dict(name="Saint Rita of Cascia", dates="1381–1457 · Umbria", match=["rita"],
  history="A wife, mother, widow and Augustinian nun of Cascia in Umbria. Tradition says she bore an unhappy marriage with patience, forgave her husband’s murderers and brought peace between feuding families before entering the convent, where she received a wound from a thorn of the crown of Christ. She is the patron of impossible causes and difficult marriages.",
  life="Most of what we know of Rita comes from tradition written down after her death, but it has been loved for centuries. She was born at Roccaporena, near Cascia, and married young to Paolo Mancini, a man given to violence. By her patience and prayer, it is said, he was changed. He was murdered in a feud, and Rita forgave his killers, but her two sons vowed revenge; she prayed that they would rather die than commit murder, and both died of illness soon after. She asked to enter the Augustinian convent of Saint Mary Magdalene at Cascia and was refused three times, until she had reconciled the families involved in the feud. In the convent she lived in prayer and obedience, and around 1442 a wound like that of a thorn opened in her forehead and stayed with her for fifteen years. Roses are blessed on her feast.",
  prayerTo="Saint Rita, you bore suffering in marriage and loss with patience, and chose forgiveness over revenge. Pray for those in hard marriages, for families divided by anger, and for all who face what seems impossible. Help me to forgive, to make peace, and to trust God with what I cannot change. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for a marriage or family you know to be in difficulty. Take the first step toward peace in a quarrel of your own.",
  meditation="She would not enter the convent until she had made peace between the families who had killed her husband. What reconciliation is God waiting for before you go further?",
  query="Saint Rita of Cascia painting"),
 "05-25": dict(name="Saint Bede the Venerable", dates="673–735 · Wearmouth and Jarrow", match=["bede"],
  history="A monk of Jarrow in Northumbria who scarcely left his monastery, yet became the most learned man in Europe. His Ecclesiastical History of the English People is the chief source for the conversion of England, and he made dating from the birth of Christ widely known. He is the only English Doctor of the Church.",
  life="Bede was born on lands belonging to the monastery of Wearmouth and was given to its abbot, Benedict Biscop, at the age of seven. He moved to the new house at Jarrow under Ceolfrith, and spent the rest of his life there, ordained deacon at nineteen and priest at thirty. He wrote of himself that his delight had always been to learn, to teach and to write. He commented on most of the Bible, wrote on time, grammar and the reckoning of Easter, composed lives of saints and poetry, and in 731 finished his history of the English Church. His disciple Cuthbert described his death: he was translating Saint John’s Gospel into English, finished the last sentence, and died on the floor of his cell singing the Glory Be, on the eve of the Ascension, 735. His relics lie in Durham Cathedral. Leo XIII named him a Doctor in 1899.",
  prayerTo="Saint Bede, you gave your whole life to learning, teaching and praising God in the cloister. Pray for England, for scholars and teachers, and for monks and nuns. Help me to love the Scriptures, to do my work faithfully to the end, and to die, as you did, giving glory to God. Amen.",
  penance="Read a chapter of Saint John’s Gospel today, the book Bede was translating as he died, and end your work with the Glory Be.",
  meditation="He compared our life to a sparrow flying through a lit hall in winter, in at one door and out at the other: we know little of what came before or what follows. What do you know of where you are going?",
  query="Venerable Bede manuscript illumination"),
 "05-26": dict(name="Saint Philip Neri", dates="1515–1595 · Florence and Rome", match=["neri"], efMatch="Neri",
  history="A Florentine who came to Rome as a young man and became its apostle, drawing people of every kind to God by friendship, prayer and laughter. He founded the Congregation of the Oratory, whose informal gatherings of prayer, Scripture and music gave the oratorio its name. Cheerful and humble, he is one of the best loved saints of Rome.",
  life="Philip was born in Florence and taught by the Dominicans at San Marco. At eighteen he was sent to learn business from a relative, but soon left for Rome, where he lived very simply, tutored two boys, and spent nights praying in the catacombs. At Pentecost 1544, in the catacomb of San Sebastiano, he felt a ball of fire enter his heart, and his heart was said to beat visibly ever after. For years he worked as a layman, talking to people in the streets and shops and founding a confraternity to care for pilgrims. Ordained in 1551, he heard confessions for hours each day. In a room above his church he gathered men to pray, read, discuss and sing, and from this came the Oratory, approved in 1575. He used jokes to keep himself and others humble. John Henry Newman brought the Oratory to England.",
  prayerTo="Saint Philip, you won souls by kindness and joy, and kept them humble with a smile. Pray for the city of Rome, for priests and confessors, and for the Oratories. Give me a cheerful heart, a love for prayer and the sacraments, and the grace not to take myself too seriously. Amen.",
  penance="Make a good confession if you can, as Philip urged his friends to do often. Do something today to cheer someone who is low, and laugh at yourself once.",
  meditation="He would wear his clothes inside out or shave half his beard so that no one would think him a saint. What would it cost you to look foolish for God?",
  query="Saint Philip Neri painting Guido Reni"),
 "05-27": dict(name="Saint Augustine of Canterbury", dates="Died c. 604 · Rome and Canterbury", match=["augustine"],
  history="A Roman monk sent by Pope Gregory the Great to bring the Gospel to the English. He landed in Kent in 597, was welcomed by King Æthelberht, whose Frankish queen Bertha was already a Christian, and baptised the king and many of his people. As first Archbishop of Canterbury he founded the Church from which English Christianity grew.",
  life="Augustine was prior of Gregory’s own monastery of Saint Andrew on the Caelian Hill when the pope chose him to lead some forty monks to England in 596. In Gaul they lost heart at the tales of a fierce people, and Augustine went back to ask that they be excused; Gregory sent him on with letters of encouragement. They landed on Thanet in 597. Bede tells us the king met them in the open air for fear of magic, listened, and allowed them to preach in Canterbury, where they prayed in the old church of Saint Martin. Æthelberht was baptised, and great numbers followed at Christmas. Augustine was consecrated bishop in Gaul, founded Christ Church and the monastery of Saints Peter and Paul at Canterbury, and set up sees at London and Rochester. Gregory advised him to purify pagan temples rather than destroy them. His talks with the bishops of the older British Church did not reach agreement. He died around 604.",
  prayerTo="Saint Augustine of Canterbury, you came to a strange land with fear and obeyed, and God made you the father of the faith in England. Pray for England and for her conversion, for bishops and missionaries, and for me, that I may obey when I am afraid and trust God to finish His work. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the conversion of England and for unity among Christians in this land. If you live near a church founded in Augustine’s time, visit it.",
  meditation="He turned back once, and was sent on again. Where have you turned back from what God asked, and are you ready to go on?",
  query="Augustine of Canterbury preaching Ethelbert painting"),
 "05-28": dict(name="Saint Augustine of Canterbury", dates="Died c. 604 · Rome and Canterbury", match=["augustine"], efMatch="Augustine",
  history="The 1962 calendar keeps the apostle of the English today. Sent by Pope Gregory the Great with a band of monks from Rome, Augustine landed in Kent in 597, baptised King Æthelberht and many of his people, and became the first Archbishop of Canterbury. The see he founded has had an unbroken line of archbishops ever since.",
  life="Gregory the Great had long wanted to send missionaries to the English; tradition says he first saw English slave boys in the Roman market and called them not Angles but angels. When he became pope he sent Augustine. The mission succeeded beyond hope. Gregory wrote to the Patriarch of Alexandria rejoicing that more than ten thousand English had been baptised at Christmas 597, and sent more monks, among them Mellitus and Paulinus, with books, vestments and relics. His letters to Augustine survive, full of practical advice: keep good customs from other churches, do not destroy the people’s temples but turn them into churches, and do not be proud of the miracles God works through you. Augustine died at Canterbury around 604 and was buried in the abbey he had founded, beside the road to Canterbury where his first converts had walked.",
  prayerTo="Saint Augustine, apostle of the English, you planted the faith in this land by obedience, patience and prayer. Pray that the faith you brought may be renewed in England, that Christians here may be one, and that I may share the Gospel humbly with those around me. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the Church in England and Wales, and offer a small sacrifice for someone you know who does not yet believe.",
  meditation="Gregory warned him not to rejoice in the wonders God worked through him, but in his name written in heaven. What do you most want to be praised for?",
  query="Saint Augustine of Canterbury painting"),
 "05-29": dict(name="Saint Mary Magdalene de’ Pazzi", dates="1566–1607 · Florence", match=["pazzi"], efMatch="Pazzi",
  history="A Carmelite nun of Florence, of one of the city’s great families, whose life of prayer was marked by long ecstasies, recorded word for word by her sisters, and by years of spiritual darkness. She burned with love for God and grief that He was so little loved, and wrote to popes and bishops pleading for the renewal of the Church.",
  life="Caterina de’ Pazzi was drawn to prayer as a child, made her first Communion at ten and soon after promised herself to God. Against her family’s wishes she entered the Carmelite monastery of Santa Maria degli Angeli in Florence in 1582, taking the name Mary Magdalene. Gravely ill as a novice, she was allowed to make her vows early on a stretcher, and then began a series of ecstasies in which she spoke aloud of the love of God, the Passion and the Holy Spirit; her sisters wrote down what she said, and these records fill several volumes. For five years she endured temptation and dryness, which she called the lions’ den. She served as mistress of novices and subprioress, and was loved for her gentleness. Tradition remembers her crying out that Love is not loved. After a long and painful illness she died in 1607, aged forty-one.",
  prayerTo="Saint Mary Magdalene de’ Pazzi, you burned with love for God and grieved that Love is not loved. Pray for contemplatives, for all in spiritual darkness, and for the renewal of the Church. Teach me to love God with my whole heart, and to persevere in prayer when it is dry. Amen.",
  penance="Spend a quarter of an hour in prayer today even if it feels dry, offering the dryness itself. Make an act of love for God for those who never think of Him.",
  meditation="Love is not loved, she is said to have cried through the cloister. What would it mean for you to love Love today?",
  query="Saint Mary Magdalene de Pazzi painting"),
 "05-31": dict(name="The Visitation of the Blessed Virgin Mary", dates="Kept since 1263; on this date since 1969", match=["visitation"],
  history="Soon after the Annunciation, Mary went in haste to the hill country of Judah to visit her kinswoman Elizabeth, who was carrying John the Baptist. At Mary’s greeting the child leapt in Elizabeth’s womb, and Elizabeth, filled with the Holy Spirit, called her blessed among women. Mary answered with the Magnificat, the song the Church sings every evening.",
  life="Saint Luke tells the story in the first chapter of his Gospel. Mary, having heard from the angel that Elizabeth had conceived in her old age, set out at once, travelling some distance to a town that tradition places at Ein Karem, near Jerusalem. Elizabeth’s words, blessed are you among women and blessed is the fruit of your womb, became part of the Hail Mary. Mary’s reply, my soul magnifies the Lord, echoes the song of Hannah and praises the God who scatters the proud and fills the hungry with good things. She stayed about three months. The Franciscans kept the feast from 1263, and Urban VI extended it to the whole Church in 1389, praying for an end to the Great Schism. It was kept on 2 July until 1969, when it moved to the last day of May, between the Annunciation and the birth of John the Baptist.",
  prayerTo="Holy Mary, you carried Christ in haste to your kinswoman, and the child in her womb leapt for joy. Bring Him to me and to all I meet today. Pray for expectant mothers and for the elderly, and teach me to magnify the Lord with you in every circumstance of my life. Amen.",
  penance="Make a visit today: to an elderly relative, an expectant mother, or someone who would value your company. Pray the Magnificat for them.",
  meditation="Mary did not wait to be visited; she went in haste. Who is waiting for you to come?",
  query="Visitation painting Ghirlandaio"),
}
